A||||14||BBBAD GT||Stranded.....Ignition I think||||9||
Z||000000||BBBAD GT||04-15-2000||09:15 PM||||Welp, this wasn't fun.  Was coming back from the drag strip (only made one run, ran 10.50 in the 1/8 mi while smoking the clutch), and the Fiero just died.  It would just just crank over and not start.  Bummed a ride back the strip, went back to the Fiero, still wouldn't start.  Got a hillbilly tow (bumper to bumper, no scratches tho) back to the strip.  I could smell raw gas from the tail pipes, so I knew it wasn't the fuel pump.  I just replaced the cap/rotor/plugs/wires/fuel filter last month, so none of them are suspect.  Tried one last time before leaving it there (I know the owners, so not that big of deal) and then it started like nothing happened.  <P>My question is has anybody's coil/moduel ever gone out on them?  If so, how common is that?  I sure don't want to be stranded again, because the Fiero was to be my daily driver/dependable car.  I don't want to be worried about this again.  <P>Any input is appreciated.<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||GT Bastard||04-15-2000||09:53 PM||||If you search in the history of the forum, I think you'll find it's fairly common for the ignition modules to go out... Strange.||||reg||1
Z||000002||BBBAD GT||04-15-2000||10:03 PM||||I think I found the answer.  Was thinking about it some more, and decided to see if that blower motor in the back is even working.  Nope, not at all.  But, what I can't understand is when I left, the car was sitting for about 45 minutes before I started it, and 20 minutes was with the deck lid open (to help get rid of that clutch smell).  Hope its not that difficult to change out....seems like everything else on the Fiero has been a pretty good challenge so far.......||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ed||04-15-2000||10:05 PM||||The module is easy to remove and test.  They do fail "all of a sudden".  It happened to me.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||86SE||04-16-2000||12:11 AM||||my coil and module both went out, about 10 seconds after one another.  but i did get about 5 more starts out of it from the first time it died and would not start.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fieroshop||04-16-2000||10:26 AM||||Hey bbbad GT, first off the blower in the back will only go on when the fan motor is on. this fan motor has nothing to do with your with your engine not running. Most likely you have on eof two problems snd might even hsvr both. First the coil could be bad and second the ignition module is bad.Check both items out very carefuuly and replace as needed.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fieroshop||04-16-2000||10:27 AM||||Hey bbbad GT, first off the blower in the back will only go on when the fan motor is on. this fan motor has nothing to do with your with your engine not running. Most likely you have on eof two problems snd might even have both. First the coil could be bad and second the ignition module is bad.Check both items out very carefuuly and replace as needed.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Homicidal||04-16-2000||12:24 PM||||It is the ignition module!  I am %99.9 sure it is!  SAME thing happened to me, all it took was $25!  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dick McHaffie||04-16-2000||04:43 PM||||Sounds like ignition module to me too.<P>I had the same problem, (except mine died on a country back road in a rain storm) and after having the coil checked, I cleaned the contacts on the ignition module, put it back together, and its been running great ever since.<P>You might be able to save a few bucks.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||04-16-2000||09:33 PM||||buy some heat sink compound at radio shack along the way. clean the distributor, apply a SMALL gob of heat sink goop to the center of the modul, and install as normal. <P>Dielectric grease and Heatsink compound ARE NOT the same. DO NOT listen to anyone who says they are. Dielectric should only be used when heatsink goo is unavailable. Even then you should remove the modul and reinstall it with heatsink goo as soon as posible. Heatsink goo will dump way more heat out of the modul than dielectric. More heat removal means longer life.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fret Naught||04-16-2000||10:00 PM||||The blower moter might be part of the problem, coils and electronic componets have two common enemies, HEAT & DIRT.  If the coil is marginal that is on its way out, heat will aid in the process. The winding gets warm, goes open ignition fails, coil cools down coil contracts and open is temp. closed again. I see it alot at work.||||reg||1
Z||000011||BBBAD GT||04-17-2000||09:53 AM||||So you think its the module?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Seriously, thanks to all for taking the time to post.  I did a search on 'modules' and found some good info.  I think I'm going to buy two modules, and just keep one in the car incase that ever happens again (I did find a spare coil in the back when I bought it, I'll test it and see if the prev owner thought it was a bad coil).  Everything on the car is pretty much bone stock, with 86k miles.  I guess I'll go with the AC/Delco part to be safe.<P>On the back blower thing, I did turn on the AC and check to see if the blower was running, it was, but only blowing on the coil.  The other tube to the alt was barely blowing (if at all).  Is there two blower fans under there, or just one and one tube maybe blocked?<P>Ogre:  Good tip on the heat sink stuff, I bet you are blue in the face explaining that everytime somebody posts a module problem (I read all the past threads).||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||04-17-2000||12:08 PM||||Unfortunatly most people don't seem to read the old posts. We're getting allot of the same questions repeated now.<P>I'm working on a large update to the cave tunnel 6 with all the HOW TO:'s as well as some other things. Hoping to send it to Cliff for posting in the next couple weeks.||||reg||1
Z||000013||BBBAD GT||04-19-2000||08:47 AM||||Yup, it was the module.  Picked up a Wells one at Autozone for 30 something bucks, and it came with a liftime warranty (not too much help when it goes on a back country road).  <P>One thing I noticed was the amount of acid that accumulated around the module, and what was most weird was the wire connections to the module that goes back to the distributor was covered in this bright green stuff.  I've never seen that before, and wondering if its GM's stock dielectic grease (doubt it, but never had a GM before). <P>Anybody have green stuff in there before?<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||theogre||04-19-2000||09:03 AM||||Green would be a copper oxide usually. Acid? there is nothing near there that should cause acid.<P>If the one you replaced was installed without heatsink goop or dielectric then it could have gotten water in there. That could have caused an electolitic condition. Then the 2 diferant metals would turn into their own power source or conduct battery current and actually eat themselves. Could make it look like acid.<P>All that greened up wire needs to be replaced. You'll want to make sure you cut a few inches either side away to make sure you stop it. You should<BR>probly replace the connectors.||||reg||1
